spent a few minutes

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.840
understanding the service, I
wanted to dig a little deeper

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.090
and start giving you
a sense of how can you

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:40.710
design your infrastructure
and best practices.

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.170
Like with so many
enterprise deployments,

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.440
it starts with networking
considerations.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.020
You want to make sure that if
you're running this managed

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:51.240
Microsoft AD domain
and AD-dependent

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.470
infrastructure with your
other infrastructure,

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.790
you think about
hybrid networking.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:01.470
Cloud VPN and interconnect are
a couple of interesting choices

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.850
for you to think through.

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:06.850
DNS resolution-- that is,
being able to resolve the name

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:08.740
of the Active Directory domain--

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:12.590
is an important consideration
in AD-based environments.

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:14.020
And so for this,
you want to think

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:15.820
about how will
that work when you

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.970
have a infrastructure
running even off Google Cloud

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:23.340
and running on Google
Cloud in different places.

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.110
You want to think about
possibly resolving

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:27.620
DNS in both directions.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:29.980
Cross-project connectivity
is another important

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.720
consideration.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:34.790
If you have AD-based
infrastructure in mind,

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:38.350
I'd recommend taking
a look at shared VPCs.

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:41.140
If you decide to
utilize shared VPCs,

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.720
ask for the managed
Microsoft AD domain

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:46.460
to be created from
the host project.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:48.920
If, for other reasons,
a hub and spoke model

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:51.170
is interesting for
you, you may want

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:54.320
to think about how will you
have network connectivity

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.780
from the infrastructure
that is running in spokes

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:59.330
and look at alternative
solutions such as VPNs

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:00.080
for those cases.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:05.330
Managed Microsoft Active
Directory seamlessly

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.350
integrates with Cloud DNS.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:09.410
So as a result of
that, VMs that you

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.600
create on authorized
networks are

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.870
able to come up and discover
Managed Microsoft AD

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.720
domains without requiring
any client side network

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.450
configuration.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.320
How does this work?

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.280
So if you look at the
box in the top left,

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.060
you'll notice that Managed
Microsoft AD creates

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:28.490
a private DNS zone
in the tenant project

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:32.190
that is hosting your Managed
AD domain controllers.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.040
Then Managed
Microsoft AD creates

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:36.950
DNS peering between
the authorized network

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:41.120
and the VPC host
AD domain and DNS.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.270
So using the box that
you see on the top right,

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.870
you are now able to
forward all the requests

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:49.160
for the AD domain's
namespace to private DNS

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:50.430
zone in the tenant project.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:55.360
If you're thinking of
running your infrastructure

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.740
in a hybrid configuration and
want to be able to resolve DNS,

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:03.280
AD-based DNS queries, from
your other infrastructure,

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.630
that is also possible.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:09.680
To do this, you would look to
create an inbound DNS policy.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:13.210
Now let's shift gears and
talk about identities.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:15.130
There is three
typical ways people

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:19.090
look to deploy AD-based
infrastructure on Google Cloud.

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:22.930
One is you can have this be
a standalone Active Directory

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:25.630
forest fully managed in Cloud.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:27.640
You can either create
Active Directory objects

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.340
or migrate them in
using other tools.

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:34.930
The second topology is a
resource AD forest model.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:38.020
In this case, your Managed
Microsoft AD domain

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:42.430
houses your Cloud VM,
computer, and service accounts.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:44.380
Your on-premises
AD domain continues

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:46.450
to host the user objects.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:49.330
And you can connect the two
using a one-way or a two-way AD

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.050
trust.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.710
The third model is a mixed
account resource forest model.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:58.730
In this case, your users who
have only cloud-only needs

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:02.220
can be fully serviced
and managed in cloud.

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.060
Your cloud
infrastructure need not

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:07.079
leap back into your on-premises
AD infrastructure, which

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.470
is typically
watched more closely

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:11.670
and deemed more sensitive.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.730
And this type of
a topology is also

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.490
very popular as a part of
a broader cloud migration

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:17.240
strategy.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:18.920
When you think about
identities, let's

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.010
put it in perspective with
separate dev and production

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:23.540
environments, which
a lot of people

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:27.880
run when it comes to running
infrastructure in the cloud.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:29.470
What you can do for
this is you could

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:33.820
choose to get two separate
Managed Microsoft AD domains

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.560
and still have a
trust going back

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:39.290
to the same on-premises user
Active Directory domain.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.320
So this way, you can
still get single sign

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:45.640
on with those same
on-premises AD accounts.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:48.580
And you can more easily
experiment and test

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:51.850
while preventing proliferation
of forgotten Active Directory

00:17:51.850 --> 00:17:53.890
accounts in your
Managed AD instances.

00:17:57.260 --> 00:17:59.360
Now let's shift the
focus to running

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:01.640
AD-dependent applications.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.410
To do this, you
obviously want to think

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.330
about where and how
your infrastructure will

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:09.830
run within the Google
Cloud resource hierarchy.

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:13.010
If you choose to
utilize our policy,

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:16.460
just know that you should
set your resource allocation

00:18:16.460 --> 00:18:18.890
constraint to allow
global so that

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:21.920
your AD-dependent infrastructure
can work with Managed Microsoft

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.390
AD.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:25.580
As you've been seeing, we
have designed flexibility

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:26.670
into the service.

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:29.600
But you can always come back and
add and remove regions or add

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:32.620
and remove authorized networks.

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:34.860
So you can get started
with what you need today.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:36.420
And the service
will evolve with you

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:41.060
when you're ready to upgrade
or enhance your infrastructure.

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:43.420
I also encourage you to
leverage our networking

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:46.660
and compute solutions to be
able to more effectively run

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.930
your AD-dependent apps.

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:52.020
Migrate for Compute Engine is
definitely a powerful offering

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:53.660
for you to think about.

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:55.410
And managed instance
[INAUDIBLE] also

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.210
tend to be really
popular, particularly

00:18:57.210 --> 00:19:01.860
with their auto-scaling
capabilities.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:04.560
I wanted to quickly talk
about a solution which

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:08.400
allows you to have your new VMs
come up and be automatically

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.820
domain joined and be able to
work with Managed Microsoft

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.210
Active Directory.

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:15.060
So I have a link on
the slide below in case

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:16.750
you'd like to go check it out.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.660
But as many of you likely
know, an effective domain

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.260
join story then makes
it possible and easy

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.260
for the infrastructure to opt
into the AD-based ecosystem

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:27.420
for authentication
and authorization.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:30.360
Let's now come to probably the
most important topic of all,

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.035
which is enhanced security.

00:19:33.035 --> 00:19:34.410
The first thing
I'd like to cover

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:36.900
is, when it comes to running
your Active Directory

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:39.400
infrastructure for you, we
take that very seriously

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:41.310
and have built in
several safeguards

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.590
to help make that be effective.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:46.800
First, the managed AD
domain controllers all

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:50.730
have private IP addresses
and run in isolated tenants.

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:54.440
They do not have direct
public internet access.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.350
Second, shielded VMs are
standard for all managed AD

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:59.360
domain controllers.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:02.070
We test OS patches
before applying them.

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:05.430
And through the patch process,
your Active Directory domain

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:07.590
remains available.

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.080
For any
Active-Directory-related service

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:13.290
accounts that the
service utilizes in AD,

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.030
the credentials
are rotated using

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.110
industry-standard techniques.

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:18.900
The service credentials
for managed AD

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:22.550
are never shared between
different instances.

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:24.950
We have procedures
in place to have

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:26.720
restricted production
access, even

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.940
for Google Cloud engineers.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:31.730
Human access is for advanced
troubleshooting only

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:34.580
and is restricted to
those who are on call.

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:38.140
On access is logged
and audited internally.

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:40.930
And access requires
validated justification.

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:43.360
Another aspect of
security comes to how

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.570
do the AD identities translate
to identity and access

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:48.250
management?

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:52.390
To show that, let me walk
you through this visual.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.100
You can see, on
the bottom right,

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:56.620
I have this managed AD instance.

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:59.800
And I've gone ahead and created
user accounts and groups

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.550
in this managed AD instance.

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:05.720
You can now choose to run a
Google Cloud directory sync

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:09.950
tool on a VM and Compute
Engine, and thereby

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:14.750
sync your Active Directory
users and passwords from here

00:21:14.750 --> 00:21:18.500
to your Cloud Identity domain.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:20.900
You can go ahead and
configure the Cloud Identity

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:24.800
domain to point to an
external identity provider.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:26.930
In this case, I have
it configured to point

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:30.800
to ADFS, which, in turn, can
go ahead and authenticate

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:35.430
the users that are housed
inside Managed Active Directory.

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:38.070
You are now all set to
utilize these managed

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:41.310
AD-hosted identities in your
Identity and Access Management

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:44.820
policies in Google Cloud.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:47.430
To show that, I will
go ahead and walk you

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.380
through this demo,
where you will notice

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:53.070
how I'm able to utilize
these managed AD-hosted users

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:56.730
to enhance authentication with
second-factor authentication

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:57.930
in Google Cloud.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:01.050
We start this demo by taking
a look at the familiar Active

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.540
Directory Administrative Center.

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:05.820
We're looking at the
contents of the objects that

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:09.150
reside inside a Google Cloud
Managed Microsoft Active

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:13.240
Directory domain, cloudaddemo.

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:16.660
One of the users that has been
created inside this Managed

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:19.180
Active Directory domain
is this user with the UPN

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:23.406
sid@cloudaddemo.joonix.net.

00:22:23.406 --> 00:22:26.580
I have preconfigured an
ongoing synchronization

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.400
from this Managed
Microsoft AD domain

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.530
to Google Cloud Identity.

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:36.040
We are now taking a look at
the Google Cloud Identity admin

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:36.730
console.

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:39.160
And you can see the same
user, which we were just

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:42.700
looking at in Active Directory,
has been synchronized up here.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:45.400
Additionally, I've
gone ahead and enabled

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:47.950
two-step verification
for this user,

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:50.080
with the second-factor
authentication being

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:53.280
provided by Google Cloud.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:55.440
One of the use cases
where we can utilize

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:58.320
the second-factor
authentication is our OS logging

00:22:58.320 --> 00:22:59.860
functionality.

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:02.050
The step by step
guide shows you how

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:07.880
to configure OS login with a
second-factor authentication.

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:10.100
As I now switch to the
Google Cloud console,

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:14.630
you can see that there is
a VM here named instance1.

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:18.650
And as I go into the
details for the specific VM,

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:21.650
you will notice that I've
gone ahead and configured

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:28.390
this VM to require OS login with
a second-factor authentication.

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:31.920
Now let's take a look at
the user's experience.

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.420
I'm now going to Google
Cloud console again.

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:36.240
But this time, as
you can see, I'm

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:41.490
logged in as the Active
Directory user Sid.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:44.480
When I now try to
SSH into this VM,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.340
you will notice that
I will get prompted

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:48.567
for a second-factor
authentication.

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:53.360
The type of second-factor
authentication

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:57.860
that I've configured in this
case is a text message OTP.

00:23:57.860 --> 00:23:59.960
So let me go ahead
and type in the code

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:01.660
that I received
on my cell phone.

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:12.420
As you can see, only upon
successfully validating

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:17.560
the second-factor authentication
was I able to SSH into this VM.

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.790
So as you can see,
there is quite a bit

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:21.860
that the base Managed
Active Directory

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:23.800
service can do for you.

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:27.560
We realize AD-based security
is a broad and deep topic.

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:29.310
And we can further
help enhance that

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:32.454
with our industry partners.

00:24:32.454 --> 00:24:35.310
Attivo Networks aims
to prevent discovery

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:37.100
of your AD infrastructure.

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:38.790
They go ahead and
distract attackers

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:42.120
with decoy AD credentials so
that your real Active Directory

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:46.020
infrastructure remains
hard to discover.

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:49.920
They do not require software
to run on AD domain controllers

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:54.780
or need admin access
to Active Directory.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:57.790
Another solution by Alsid
disrupts the dynamic

00:24:57.790 --> 00:25:00.940
of several cyber threats by
exposing misconfigurations

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:04.360
and detecting attacks
before widespread infection.

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:07.420
The Alsid solution
helps locate and fix

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.810
your existing weaknesses and
is continuously uncovering

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:12.175
and fixing new attack pathways.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.990
In addition to
Alsid and Attivo, we

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:19.940
have other industry
partners which

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:22.550
are all set to go ahead and
meet your Active Directory

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:26.720
needs across the board,
from Citrix, Itopia

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:28.370
Semperis, and Stealthbits.

00:25:33.012 --> 00:25:34.720
I wanted to thank you
for taking the time

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:37.390
today to learn more about
our Managed Active Directory

00:25:37.390 --> 00:25:40.300
service and understand how
this can go ahead and help

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:43.120
you empower run your
AD-dependent workloads more

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:45.590
effectively on Google Cloud.

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:49.530
Thank you for joining me, and
hope you have a wonderful day.

